course: books
lesson: 7
title: Alice in Wonderland, chapter IV, part 1: Stuck in the White Rabbit's house
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The White Rabbit comes back hunting for his lost fan and gloves, mistakes Alice for his housemaid Mary Ann, and sends her running home to fetch them; she is too startled to argue.
explain: In his neat little house she finds the fan and gloves, but also a bottle with no label, and, sure that something interesting will happen, she drinks it.
explain: She grows and grows until she fills the whole room, with one arm out of the window and one foot up the chimney, and she wonders half sadly, half curiously what will become of her.
explain: The Rabbit can't get in at the door, so he tries the window; Alice snatches at the air, hears a shriek and breaking glass, and guesses he has fallen into a cucumber-frame.
explain: The Rabbit and his gardener Pat argue about the giant arm in the window, and a second snatch brings two shrieks and more broken glass: a scary situation turned into a funny one.
fact: The Rabbit mutters "The Duchess! The Duchess! ... She'll get me executed, as sure as ferrets are ferrets!"; "executed" means put to death, and ferrets are small hunting animals that chase rabbits.
fact: The great hall, the glass table and the little door have "vanished completely".
fact: The Rabbit calls out "Why, Mary Ann, what are you doing out here?" and orders her to fetch "a pair of gloves and a fan"; Alice says "He took me for his housemaid".
fact: The little house has a bright brass plate on the door reading "W. RABBIT", and Alice goes in without knocking and hurries upstairs, afraid of meeting the real Mary Ann and being turned out.
fact: She imagines Dinah sending her "on messages" (errands), and thinks they would not let Dinah stay in the house if she began "ordering people about like that".
fact: In a tidy room she finds a fan and "two or three pairs of tiny white kid gloves", and near the looking-glass (mirror) a little bottle with no label.
fact: She drinks because "I know something interesting is sure to happen" "whenever I eat or drink anything".
fact: Before she has drunk half the bottle her head presses against the ceiling; she ends with one arm out of the window and one foot up the chimney.
fact: She thinks "It was much pleasanter at home", but also "it's rather curious, you know, this sort of life!", and says "There ought to be a book written about me".
fact: She jokes "I'm grown up now", because there is "no room to grow up any more here", and wonders if she will never get older: no old age, but lessons for ever; then she answers herself: "How can you learn lessons in here? Why, there's hardly room for you, and no room at all for any lesson-books!"
fact: The door opens inwards and Alice's elbow is pressed against it, so the Rabbit cannot open it.
fact: Alice is now "about a thousand times as large as the Rabbit", yet she "trembled till she shook the house" when she hears him coming, forgetting she has no reason to be afraid.
fact: Her snatch out of the window brings "a little shriek and a fall, and a crash of broken glass"; a cucumber-frame is a low glass box that keeps young plants warm, like a tiny greenhouse.
fact: Pat says he is "Digging for apples, yer honour!" ("yer honour" = sir), which is nonsense, since apples grow on trees; the Rabbit calls him "you goose" (silly), Pat says the thing in the window is "an arm", and the Rabbit orders him to take it away: "Do as I tell you, you coward!"
fact: After the second snatch Alice thinks "I'm sure I don't want to stay in here any longer!"
fact: Carroll makes fun of Pat's Irish way of speaking ("arrum" for arm); laughing at people for their accents was a common old habit, and it is wrong and unkind today.
fact: Old words in today's English: "lest" = in case, "directly" = at once, "as a last resource" = as the last thing left to try, "no sort of chance" = no chance at all.
exercise: What was the White Rabbit looking for at the start of chapter IV? | The fan and the white kid gloves | The golden key and the little door | His watch and his waistcoat | A box of comfits and a thimble
exercise: Who was the Rabbit afraid would get him executed? | The Duchess | The Queen of Hearts | The old Dodo | Mary Ann the maid
exercise: Here "executed" means... | Put to death | Sent to school | Made to work hard | Locked in a room
exercise: What had happened to the great hall and the little door? | They had vanished completely | They had been locked up tight | They had grown very much larger | They had filled up with tears
exercise: What name did the Rabbit call Alice? | Mary Ann | Little Mabel | Little Ada | Dinah the cat
exercise: Who did the Rabbit think Alice was? | His housemaid | His little daughter | The angry Duchess | A lost mouse from the pool
exercise: Why did Alice not explain the Rabbit's mistake? | She was so frightened that she just ran off | She wanted to steal something from his house | She thought being a maid would be good fun | She was too tall to talk to such a small rabbit
exercise: What was written on the brass plate on the door? | "W. RABBIT" | "MARY ANN" | "DRINK ME" | "THE DUCHESS"
exercise: How did Alice go into the Rabbit's house? | Without knocking, hurrying upstairs | By ringing the bell and waiting politely | Through the window at the back | Down the chimney like a sweep
exercise: Why did Alice hurry upstairs? | In case she met the real Mary Ann and was turned out | Because the Rabbit had told her the gloves were there | Because she heard Dinah meowing in an upstairs room | Because the stairs were about to fall down behind her
exercise: Who did Alice imagine sending her on messages next? | Dinah | Her sister | The Mouse | The Dodo
exercise: How many pairs of gloves were on the table in the window? | Two or three pairs | Exactly one pair | Ten pairs or more | None at all, not a single one
exercise: Where did Alice find the little bottle? | Near the looking-glass | Under the bed | On the stairs | In the cold ashes of the fireplace
exercise: What was different about this bottle? | It had no label at all | It said "POISON" on it in red | It was made of solid gold | It was much bigger than the first
exercise: Why did Alice drink from the bottle anyway? | Something interesting always happened when she ate or drank | The Rabbit had told her it would make her the right size | She was terribly thirsty after swimming in the pool | She thought it was a bottle of the Rabbit's medicine
exercise: How much of the bottle had she drunk when her head hit the ceiling? | Not even half | The whole bottle | Just one drop | Three bottles
exercise: Where did Alice put one arm and one foot? | An arm out of the window and a foot up the chimney | An arm up the chimney and a foot out of the door | Both arms out of the window and a foot on the bed | One arm under the table and one foot halfway down the stairs
exercise: What did Alice think there ought to be about her? | A book written about her | A statue of her in the garden | A song sung about her | A prize given to her
exercise: Why did Alice say "I'm grown up now"? | There was no room to grow up any more in the room | She had just had her birthday down the hole | The Rabbit had told her she was now a grown-up | She had learnt all of her lessons and so could stop doing them
exercise: What good thing did Alice see in never getting older? | Never being an old woman | Never having lessons | Never going to bed | Never being hungry or thirsty again
exercise: Why could the Rabbit not open the door? | It opened inwards and Alice's elbow was against it | It was locked and the Rabbit had lost its key | Alice had pushed the table up against the door | The door was far too heavy for a little rabbit
exercise: About how much larger than the Rabbit was Alice now? | About a thousand times | About twice | About ten times | About the same size as him
exercise: What did Alice guess the Rabbit had fallen into? | A cucumber-frame | A pool of tears | A rabbit-hole | A barrel full of rain water
exercise: What is a cucumber-frame? | A low glass box that keeps young plants warm | A wooden box for keeping cucumbers in the larder | A picture frame with a cucumber painted on it | A fence around a field where cucumbers grow wild
exercise: What did Pat say he was doing? | Digging for apples | Picking pears | Feeding the pigs | Fetching water from the well
exercise: What did Pat say was in the window? | An arm | A giant | A ladder | A broom
exercise: How many shrieks did Alice hear after her second snatch? | Two | One | Three | None
exercise: Thinking back: when and why did the Rabbit drop the fan and gloves? | In the hall, frightened when giant Alice spoke to him | In the pool, when he was swimming with the Mouse | On the bank, while the animals were running the race | In the well, as he fell down past the shelves
exercise: Thinking back: what had the fan done to Alice when she held it? | Made her shrink | Made her grow | Made her sneeze | Made her sleepy
test: Harvey goes: "Alice broke into the Rabbit's house to nick his stuff." ("nick" = steal.) Put him right. | The Rabbit sent her there to fetch his fan and gloves | She went there to have a rest after the race | She went there to find Dinah, who was lost | The Mouse told her to hide in the house
test: Amara, Gen Z: "Alice drank a random bottle AGAIN? The girl is not learning, it's giving chaos." ("it's giving" = it looks like.) What was her reason this time? | Something interesting always happened when she ate or drank | She checked the label and it said it was safe to drink | The Rabbit had left a note saying "DRINK ME" beside it | She thought it would turn her into the real Mary Ann
test: What would have happened if the door had opened outwards? | The Rabbit could have opened it and come in | Alice would have fallen down the stairs | The house would have fallen down at once | Nothing would be any different from what happened at all
test: Why is it funny that Alice trembles "till she shook the house"? | She is huge, so her fear shakes the whole house, yet she fears a tiny rabbit | She is so cold that her teeth are chattering against the walls of the house | The house is made of jelly and wobbles whenever anyone moves | The Rabbit is shaking the house from outside to frighten her away
test: Rory from Belfast: "Wee Pat's an eejit, diggin' for apples, so he is." ("wee" = little, "eejit" = idiot.) Why is "digging for apples" nonsense? | Apples grow on trees, not under the ground | Apples are far too big for a rabbit's garden | Pat had no spade to dig with in the garden | Nobody in Wonderland likes eating apples
test: True or false: Alice wants to stay in the Rabbit's house. | False: "I'm sure I don't want to stay in here any longer!" | True: she likes it and wants to live there for ever | True: she is waiting for Dinah to come and find her | False: she wants to go straight on to the Duchess's house instead of staying
test: Grandad, grumpy: "Back in my day a girl that size would've been in the circus." What does Alice herself think about her strange life? | Much pleasanter at home, and yet rather curious | It is the best day of her whole life so far | It is so boring she wants to fall asleep | It is just a normal day like any other
test: Why does Alice say "How can you learn lessons in here?"? | There is hardly room for her, and none for lesson-books | The Rabbit has thrown all her lesson-books away | She has forgotten how to read ever since she fell down the hole | Lessons are not allowed in Wonderland at all
test: Why does Alice think there must be a lot of cucumber-frames? | She hears broken glass after both snatches | She can see a row of them from the window | The Rabbit told her he grows cucumbers | Pat shouted out that he had fallen right in among the cucumbers
test: Jess, rough: "Do as I tell you, you coward!" Who says this? | The Rabbit, to Pat | Alice, to the Rabbit | Pat, to the Rabbit | Mary Ann, to Alice
test: Looking back: in chapter II the Rabbit ran away in fright from giant Alice. How does he treat her at the start of chapter IV? | He orders her about, thinking she is his maid | He runs away again as soon as he sees her | He bows and says "If you please, miss" | He politely asks her the way to the Duchess's house in the wood
test: Looking back: what has Alice now drunk or eaten, in order, since the start of the book? | DRINK ME bottle, EAT ME cake, and now the Rabbit's bottle | EAT ME cake, DRINK ME bottle, and now a piece of mushroom | Comfits, DRINK ME bottle, and now the Rabbit's little cake | Marmalade, EAT ME cake, and now the DRINK ME bottle
test: Looking back to chapter I: Alice checked the first bottle for "poison". What does she check this time? | Nothing; there is no label and she just drinks | She checks it for "poison" just the same | She asks the Rabbit's maid what is in it before she drinks | She smells it and tastes only one drop
test: How has Alice's way of thinking changed since chapter I? | She now expects magic whenever she eats or drinks | She is now far more careful than she was before | She now thinks nothing strange ever happens | She is now sure she will never grow again
test: ★★★ First snatch: "a little shriek". Second snatch: "two little shrieks". How many shrieks did Alice hear altogether? | Three | Two | Four | Only one
test: ★★★ "I'm grown up now," Alice says. What are the two meanings of "grown up" in her joke? | Grown big in size, and become an adult | Grown taller, and grown older than her sister | Grown up a chimney, and grown out of a window | Grown sad, and grown bored of lessons
test: ★★★ Read carefully: was Alice right to be afraid of the Rabbit? | No: she was a thousand times larger and had no reason to fear him | Yes: the Rabbit was carrying a sword and a very large stick | Yes: the Rabbit had already caught her once in the hall | No: the Rabbit had promised her she was safe in his house
exam: Identify the person for whom the White Rabbit mistook the protagonist. | His housemaid, Mary Ann | His sister, Mabel | The Duchess | The Queen
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the protagonist consumed the contents of the unlabelled bottle. | Her expectation that something of interest would ensue | An instruction from the White Rabbit to do so | A desire to assume the identity of Mary Ann | The thirst occasioned by her swim in the pool
exam: It is given that the door of the chamber opened inwards. Determine the reason the Rabbit's attempt to enter proved a failure. | The protagonist's elbow was pressed hard against it | The door had been secured by a lock of brass | The Rabbit lacked the strength to turn the handle | Pat had obstructed the doorway with a cucumber-frame
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the protagonist's dimensions relative to the Rabbit. | Approximately one thousand times as large | Approximately twice as large | Approximately equal in size | Approximately ten times as large
